esents that we made and and bought and  gave to each other to the food that we  put on the table to the the sense that  we were all here together once again  there were other family gatherings you  know Easter was another big time but  Christmas was particularly important I I  would say throughout my childhood and  well into my 20s  um  throughout graduate school so these are  some of the things I could talk for  hours and hours and hours about  different particular incidents or you  know other things that we did that were  particularly uh interesting or relevant  or poignant but I'm going to cut it  short here and just say that  it's because of the goodness the  the  love and the companionship and the  mutual interest and all of these other  things that I got to enjoy the wonderful  time of Christmas  that  um lacking that since the family has  dispersed and you know people are living  in different places and are busy and the  oldest generation is all gone my  grandparents generation and even you  know  my aunts and uncles  um my mother died a long time ago Uncle  aim died during the covid epidemic not  of covid but of other things even that  generation has kind of hollowed out  we don't have that as a a shared  experience anymore and I remember it and  Christmas music and festivities remind  me of it and I'm not complaining I was  extraordinarily lucky and blessed to  have had that experience year after year  after year  but the lack of it  does sometimes you know weigh upon me  and you know I do have the the fortune  of being able to look back on all of  these memories and spend some time  perhaps sitting with some eggnog in  front of a fire somewhere else  reminiscing about my my family and this  wonderful  world of the past not lost to me but no  longer something that I get to  participate in in the present  so that is my little reminiscence about  Christmas time among the lemuries
 

